  Quiet reach, DM / AMI = 1.76x, where the sqrt(f)
  arithmetic predicts 0.71x the other way because DM
  occupies twice the band.  The arithmetic measures the wrong
  thing: with ideal AGC the section is closed by DISPERSION, not
  by flat loss.  The monostable classifier decides on transition
  timing RATIOS (0.75 cell), which a tilted channel scales without
  breaking; AMI decides on amplitude against a peak-derived
  threshold, which is what dispersion attacks.

  The two limits are independent.  DM buys section length and pays
  in impulse: about 5-6x AMI's breaks per second, and that ratio is
  FLAT with distance, so no shorter section buys it back.  This is
  line-codes.md §4 (AMI ~1/6 the breaks) reproduced at MF2's rate on
  the buried pair for the first time.

